{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong level bias (underprediction): model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially in rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift to the whole path; the required uplift tends to grow with horizon (small at 1–3d, large by 10–30d).",
    "Trend/acceleration miss (lag): when the last 1–2 weeks show a clear uptrend, the model reacts too slowly and produces a flatter path. Add a trend-following term proportional to recent slope (e.g., 5–10d momentum) to steepen the early-to-mid horizon.",
    "Regime dependence: in sustained rallies, errors compound over time (underestimation increases with horizon). Use a horizon-dependent correction (increasing uplift with day-ahead index) rather than a constant offset.",
    "Post-peak behavior: after a local high, the model often predicts too much mean reversion/decline versus outcomes that stay elevated longer. Reduce mean-reversion strength when spot is above recent moving averages and volatility is elevated.",
    "Late-horizon directional error: forecasts frequently drift back toward ~70–77 even when the realized path remains in the 80s. Anchor long-horizon levels more to recent realized regime (e.g., 20–30d MA) and less to a fixed long-run mean."
  ]
}
